<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">St. Mary's Church, Lübeck</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1250–<a href="/years/1350">1350</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Marienkirche, Lübeck</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">St. Mary's Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> is a brick Gothic church built by the city's merchants between about 1250 and 1350 beside the town hall. Its soaring nave became the model for many churches around the Baltic and expressed the wealth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. Badly damaged in a bombing raid in 1942, it has since been restored.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-marys-church-lubeck.jpg" alt="St. Mary's Church, Lübeck" width="320" height="278" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carsten Steger · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_image_of_St._Mary%27s_Church,_L%C3%BCbeck_(view_from_the_west).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>St. Mary's stands on the highest point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>'s old town island, within the Hanseatic merchants' quarter, and served as the main parish church of the citizens and the city council. After 1160 a wooden church was built on the site and first documented in 1170 as a market church. It was replaced at the end of the 12th century by a Romanesque brick church. The sixth pair of pillars in the nave, from around 1200, is a remnant of that building.</p><p>The Gothic church was built as a three-aisled basilica with side chapels, an ambulatory with radiating chapels, and vestibules like the arms of a transept. Its design followed the stone cathedrals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. The west front has two towers, each over 124 metres high with their weather vanes, and the central nave, 38.5 metres high, has the tallest brick vault in the world.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>St. Mary's is the model of north German Brick Gothic and set the standard for about 70 other churches in the Baltic region, among them St. Nicholas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a> and St. Nicholas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wismar">Wismar</a>. It is called the mother church of brick Gothic and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> city centre, was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 1987. The city had been the de facto capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>.</p><p>The church was richly endowed by the council, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> and families, and at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it had 38 altars and 65 benefices. Surviving medieval works include a bronze font by Hans Apengeter of 1337 and a tabernacle of 1479 made of about a thousand bronze parts. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> altarpiece of 1518 shows the life of the Virgin Mary in 26 scenes. The church was Lutheran from the Church Order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-bugenhagen">Johannes Bugenhagen</a> in 1531.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Its musical tradition was shaped by the organists Franz Tunder and his son-in-law Dieterich Buxtehude, who served from 1668 to 1707. In 1705 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a>, aged twenty, walked more than 400 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnstadt">Arnstadt</a> to hear Buxtehude and stayed nearly three months.</p><p>In an air raid by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> on 28 and 29 March 1942 the church was almost completely destroyed by fire, along with about a fifth of the city centre. The great organ, the Danse Macabre organ and works by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernt-notke">Bernt Notke</a> were lost. Heat from the fire dislodged plaster and revealed medieval wall paintings. In 1948 Dietrich Fey was given the task of restoring them and hired the painter Lothar Malskat, who invented some of the paintings. This became the largest counterfeit art scandal after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>: Malskat reported it in 1952 and both men received prison sentences in 1954. The church was rebuilt between 1947 and 1959.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “St. Mary's Church, Lübeck” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wealth came from trade in herring, salt, grain, furs and timber, visible in the Holstentor and <b>St. Mary's Church</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-lubeck">Free and Hanseatic City of Lübeck</a></li></ul></section>
